<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dinefwr Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dinefwr Castle stands on a ridge above the river Tywi near Llandeilo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Welsh tradition made Dinefwr the chief seat of the kings of Deheubarth, and the stone castle was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-gruffydd">the Lord Rhys</a> or his sons in the 12th and 13th centuries. After the English conquest it passed to the crown, and the surrounding parkland is now in the care of the National Trust.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinefwr-castle.jpg" alt="Dinefwr Castle" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Phillips · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinefwr_castle_at_sunrise.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The ruined castle, also known as Old Dynevor Castle, lies on a ridge on the northern bank of the Towy, with a steep drop of one hundred feet to the river. A fortified entrance with a restored length of battlement leads into the space enclosed by the walls, from which stairs climb to the battlements and towers; a narrow spiral staircase reaches a high tower looking over the deer park to the north and the valley to the south and west. The keep is entered through the cellar at its base, and a well and several garderobes survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a> first built a castle here, though no remains of that age have been dated, and that it became the chief seat of his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a>. Rhys ap Gruffydd, ruler of Deheubarth from 1155 to 1197, is thought to have rebuilt it. Giraldus Cambrensis tells how a follower of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>, sent to scout the castle, was led by a Welsh cleric along the hardest route, and the planned attack was abandoned.</p><p>Rhys's son Rhys Gryg, unable to resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a> of Gwynedd, dismantled the castle, but Llywelyn restored it and held it until his death in 1240; the earliest parts of the present castle are thought to date from this time. Rhys ap Maredudd helped King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> take Dinefwr in 1277, apparently on a promise that he would have it, but Edward did not keep the promise and had him executed in 1291.</p><p>In English hands the castle was burnt during the rebellion of Llywelyn Bren in 1316, given in 1317 to the king's favourite Hugh Despenser, and besieged without success by the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> in 1403. Sir Rhys ap Thomas rebuilt it extensively late in the 15th century; after his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-gruffydd">Rhys ap Gruffydd</a> was executed for treason in 1531 the crown confiscated it, though the family later recovered it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>When Newton House was built nearby in 1660, the keep was adapted as a summer house, which burned down in the 18th century. The castle is a Grade I listed building, owned by the Wildlife Trust of South and West Wales and managed by Cadw, which since 2024 has used the Welsh name Castell Dinefwr in English. It lies within Dinefwr Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dinefwr Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
